## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design crash‑consistent data paths in persistent memory, a common production issue when power failures occur during in‑flight operations.

## Key Concepts

- Durability guarantees for PMEM writes
- Memory ordering: release/acquire, fences, cacheline flushes
- Recovery via redo log and slot sequence counters
- ABA avoidance in a concurrent ring
- Testing with fault injection and crash replay

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo-code: enqueue/dequeue with PMEM flush/fence
// Slot: { data, seq, valid }
void enqueue(T v){
  size_t t = tail.fetch_add(1);
  Slot *s = &ring[t % CAP];
  s->data = v; // write data
  clwb(&s->data, sizeof(T));
  fence(); // ensure data persists
  s->seq = t; // publish with durable tail update (release)
  clwb(&s->seq, sizeof(size_t));
  fence();
  // publish tail already happens via atomic tail update
}

bool dequeue(T *out){
  size_t h0 = head.load();
  Slot *s = &ring[h0 % CAP];
  if (s->seq != h0) return false;
  *out = s->data;
  s->valid = false;
  clwb(&s->valid, sizeof(bool));
  fence();
  head.fetch_add(1);
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle power loss during the publish vs data write steps?
- How would you validate recovery correctness across multiple restart scenarios?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of low-level synchronization primitives, memory ordering, and practical reuse of barriers without locks.

## Key Concepts
- Atomic fetch_add and compare_exchange
- Memory_order_acquire/release or relaxed with correct ordering
- Sense-reversing barrier for reuse
- Spin-wait costs and NUMA locality

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { atomic_int count; atomic_int sense; int N; } barrier_t;

void barrier_init(barrier_t *b, int N){
  atomic_init(&b->count, 0);
  atomic_init(&b->sense, 0);
  b->N = N;
}

void barrier_wait(barrier_t *b, int *local_sense){
  int my_sense = !*local_sense;
  if (atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&b->count, 1, memory_order_acq_rel) + 1 == b->N){
    atomic_store_explicit(&b->count, 0, memory_order_relaxed);
    atomic_xor_explicit(&b->sense, 1, memory_order_release);
  } else {
    while (atomic_load_explicit(&b->sense, memory_order_acquire) != *local_sense){
      // spin
    }
  }
  *local_sense = my_sense;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for dynamic N? 
- How to avoid energy waste on long waits?

